{"reductionName":"Garey-Johnson Reduction","reductionDefinition":"Garey and Johnson Reduction converts 3SAT to a set of elements, and constraints of a 3-dimensional matching problem. The varibles are represented by wheels of 2 constraints for each clause a variable is in. The clauses are each mapped to a group of contraints all sharing two elements, with the third attaching to a varible gadget. Garbage collection gadgets are than created as constrains that assure any unincluded elements outside of the clause gadget are included in a matching.","source":"Garey, M. R. and David S. Johnson. \u201CComputers and Intractability: A Guide to the Theory of NP-Completeness.\u201D (1978).","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_SAT3/ReduceTo/NPC_DM3/GareyJohnson.cs","contributors":["Caleb Eardley"],"cost":"Quadratic","reductionType":"ComponentDesign","reductionTypes":["ComponentDesign"],"complexityBucket":"Polynomial","complexity":"O(n^2), n ~ |SAT3.literals|","gadgetMap":{},"reductionFrom":{"problemName":"3SAT","problemLink":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_satisfiability_problem#3-satisfiability","formalDefinition":"3SAT = {\u03A6 | \u03A6 is a satisfiable Boolean formula in 3CNF}","problemDefinition":"3SAT is the Boolean satisfiability problem restricted to formulas in conjunctive normal form with at most three literals per clause. It asks whether there is an assignment of True/False values to the variables of \u03A6 that makes the whole formula True.","inputDescription":"\u03A6, a Boolean formula in 3-CNF","outputDescription":"True or False, whether \u03A6 is satisfiable","contributors":["Kaden Marchetti"],"source":"Karp, Richard M. Reducibility among combinatorial problems. Complexity of computer computations. Springer, Boston, MA, 1972. 85-103.","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_SAT3/SAT3_Class.cs","sourceLink":"https://cgi.di.uoa.gr/~sgk/teaching/grad/handouts/karp.pdf","defaultInstance":"(x1 | x2 | x3) \u0026 (!x1 | !x2 | !x3) \u0026 (!x1 | x2 | x3) \u0026 (x1 | !x2 | x3)","instanceFormat":"Boolean formula in 3-CNF. Clauses joined by \u0027\u0026\u0027, literals within a clause joined by \u0027|\u0027, negation prefix \u0027!\u0027 written directly before the name. Variable names start with a letter or \u0027_\u0027 and contain only letters, digits and \u0027_\u0027. When there are several clauses, each clause with more than one literal must be in parentheses. Each clause has at most 3 literals. Example: (x1 | x2 | x3) \u0026 (!x1 | !x2 | !x3) \u0026 (!x1 | x2 | x3) \u0026 (x1 | !x2 | x3)","certificateFormat":"Comma-separated variable:Boolean pairs, optionally wrapped in parentheses. Booleans must be capitalized True/False (T/F also accepted); \u0027:\u0027 or \u0027=\u0027 may be used as the separator. List every variable you are assigning. Example: (x1:True,x2:False,x3:True)","defaultSolver":{"solverName":"3SAT Backtracking","solverDefinition":"This is a O(2^n) solution algorithm for the 3SAT problem which implements a back tracking algorithm to find an exact assignment boolean assignment of variables to satisfy the problem instance.","source":"","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_SAT3/Solvers/Sat3BacktrackingSolver.cs","contributors":["David Lindeman","Kaden Marchetti"],"timerHasExpired":false,"solverType":"Backtracking","solverTypes":["Backtracking"],"complexityBucket":"Exponential","timeComplexity":{"Function":"2^n * |phi|","Description":", n = number of variables, |phi| = formula size","VariableNames":["n","phi"]}},"defaultVerifier":{"verifierName":"3SAT Solution Verifier","verifierDefinition":"This is a verifier for 3SAT. It takes the certificate from the user and validates that every clause contains a true literal","source":"","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_SAT3/Verifiers/SAT3Verifier.cs","contributors":["Kaden Marchetti"],"certificate":""},"defaultVisualization":{"visualizationName":"3SAT Visualization","visualizationDefinition":"This is a default visualization for 3SAT","source":"","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_SAT3/Visualizations/Sat3DefaultVisualization.cs","contributors":["Kaden Marchetti"],"visualizationType":"BooleanSatisfiability","visualizationTypes":["BooleanSatisfiability"],"pictureType":"GroupedGraph","solver":{"solverName":"3SAT Backtracking","solverDefinition":"This is a O(2^n) solution algorithm for the 3SAT problem which implements a back tracking algorithm to find an exact assignment boolean assignment of variables to satisfy the problem instance.","source":"","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_SAT3/Solvers/Sat3BacktrackingSolver.cs","contributors":["David Lindeman","Kaden Marchetti"],"timerHasExpired":false,"solverType":"Backtracking","solverTypes":["Backtracking"],"timeComplexity":{"Function":"2^n * |phi|","Description":", n = number of variables, |phi| = formula size","VariableNames":["n","phi"]},"spaceComplexity":null,"complexityBucket":"Exponential"}},"complexityClass":"NPComplete","problemType":"Logic","problemTypes":["Logic"],"problemVariants":["Decision"],"instance":"(x1 | x2 | x3) \u0026 (!x1 | !x2 | !x3) \u0026 (!x1 | x2 | x3) \u0026 (x1 | !x2 | x3)","wikiName":"","clauses":[["x1","x2","x3"],["!x1","!x2","!x3"],["!x1","x2","x3"],["x1","!x2","x3"]],"literals":["x1","x2","x3","!x1","!x2","!x3","!x1","x2","x3","x1","!x2","x3"]},"reductionTo":{"problemName":"3-Dimensional Matching","problemLink":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3-dimensional_matching","formalDefinition":"{\u003CM,X,Y,Z\u003E | M is a subset of X*Y*Z,|X|=|Y|=|Z| and a subset of M, M\u0027, exists, where |M\u0027| = |A|,|B|,|C|, and no two elements of M\u0027 agree in any cooridinate}","problemDefinition":"3-Dimensional Matching is when, given 3 equally sized sets, X, Y, and Z, and a set of constraints M, being a subset of XxYxZ, are you able to select a set of constraints which contain each element of X, Y, and Z in one and only one 3-tuple.","inputDescription":"X, Y, and Z, three equally sized sets, and M, a set of 3-tuple constraints over X x Y x Z","outputDescription":"True or False, whether a subset of M exists that matches every element of X, Y, and Z exactly once","source":"Karp, Richard M. Reducibility among combinatorial problems. Complexity of computer computations. Springer, Boston, MA, 1972. 85-103.","sourceFile":"Problems/NPComplete/NPC_DM3/DM3_Class.cs","sourceLink":"https://cgi.di.uoa.gr/~sgk/teaching/grad/handouts/karp.pdf","defaultInstance":"{Paul,Sally,Dave}{Madison,Austin,Bob}{Chloe,Frank,Jake}{Paul,Madison,Chloe}{Paul,Austin,Jake}{Sally,Bob,Chloe}{Sally,Madison,Frank}{Dave,Austin,Chloe}{Dave,Bob,Chloe}","instance":"{x_x1_0,x_x1_1,x_x1_2,x_x1_3,x_x2_0,x_x2_1,x_x2_2,x_x2_3,x_x3_0,x_x3_1,x_x3_2,x_x3_3,x_clause_0,x_clause_1,x_clause_2,x_clause_3,x_garb_0,x_garb_1,x_garb_2,x_garb_3,x_garb_4,x_garb_5,x_garb_6,x_garb_7}{y_x1_0,y_x1_1,y_x1_2,y_x1_3,y_x2_0,y_x2_1,y_x2_2,y_x2_3,y_x3_0,y_x3_1,y_x3_2,y_x3_3,y_clause0,y_clause1,y_clause2,y_clause3,y_garb_0,y_garb_1,y_garb_2,y_garb_3,y_garb_4,y_garb_5,y_garb_6,y_garb_7}{z_x1_0,z_!x1_0,z_x1_1,z_!x1_1,z_x1_2,z_!x1_2,z_x1_3,z_!x1_3,z_x2_0,z_!x2_0,z_x2_1,z_!x2_1,z_x2_2,z_!x2_2,z_x2_3,z_!x2_3,z_x3_0,z_!x3_0,z_x3_1,z_!x3_1,z_x3_2,z_!x3_2,z_x3_3,z_!x3_3}{x_x1_0,y_x1_0,z_x1_0}{x_x1_0,y_x1_1,z_!x1_0}{x_x1_1,y_x1_1,z_x1_1}{x_x1_1,y_x1_2,z_!x1_1}{x_x1_2,y_x1_2,z_x1_2}{x_x1_2,y_x1_3,z_!x1_2}{x_x1_3,y_x1_3,z_x1_3}{x_x1_3,y_x1_0,z_!x1_3}{x_x2_0,y_x2_0,z_x2_0}{x_x2_0,y_x2_1,z_!x2_0}{x_x2_1,y_x2_1,z_x2_1}{x_x2_1,y_x2_2,z_!x2_1}{x_x2_2,y_x2_2,z_x2_2}{x_x2_2,y_x2_3,z_!x2_2}{x_x2_3,y_x2_3,z_x2_3}{x_x2_3,y_x2_0,z_!x2_3}{x_x3_0,y_x3_0,z_x3_0}{x_x3_0,y_x3_1,z_!x3_0}{x_x3_1,y_x3_1,z_x3_1}{x_x3_1,y_x3_2,z_!x3_1}{x_x3_2,y_x3_2,z_x3_2}{x_x3_2,y_x3_3,z_!x3_2}{x_x3_3,y_x3_3,z_x3_3}{x_x3_3,y_x3_0,z_!x3_3}{x_clause_0,y_clause0,z_x1_0}{x_clause_0,y_clause0,z_x2_0}{x_clause_0,y_clause0,z_x3_0}{x_clause_1,y_clause1,z_!x1_0}{x_clause_1,y_clause1,z_!x2_0}{x_clause_1,y_clause1,z_!x3_0}{x_clause_2,y_clause2,z_!x1_1}{x_clause_2,y_clause2,z_x2_1}{x_clause_2,y_clause2,z_x3_1}{x_clause_3,y_clause3,z_x1_1}{x_claus